3) what can be clicked/interacted with reveal nothing in source view. 


  Commands used in session:
        "gobuster dir -u http://[10.113.146.201] -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt -x php,html,txt -t 10"
gobuster revealed a few directories. Some was already known. 
One that stands out is "custom".

          "gobuster dir -u http://10.113.146.201/custom/ -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt -x php,html,txt -t 10"

Scanning for any subdirs revealed a "users.bak" file.

The users.bak file contained a name, admin, and hashcode, translating to bulldog19. Credentials do not work 
on the SSH server.


After seeing there were not any login directory pages I missed, I ran nmap again to check for any missed open ports.

  Commands used in session:
      "nmap -p- --min-rate 1000 10.113.146.201"

This revealed port 8765, ultraseek-hhtp.

Accessing this port revealed an admin login page, and viewing the page source, revealed a directory and name.

      ""Example=/auth/dontforget.bak"

The directory revealed an xml text. Using it to test whether or not the admin page parses such text, I went on to make
an xml file to parse the .bak file, revealing a private SSH key. 

The name revealed was Barry.

Using ssh2john and john to crack the key enables us to get in as barry.

A simple ls -la search would reveal that joe has a live_log. 

A lack of a specified path on the command that joe is running was then used to perform a SUID path hijack to get root. 











